753	    /**
754	     * transcriptMode on the listView decides when to scroll back to the first item.
755	     * The value we have by default, TRANSCRIPT_MODE_ALWAYS_SCROLL, means that on every new search,
756	     * (actually, on any change to the listview's adapter items)
757	     * scroll is reset to the bottom, which makes sense as we want the most relevant search results
758	     * to be visible first (searching for "ab" after "a" should reset the scroll).
759	     * However, when updating an existing result set (for instance to remove a record, add a tag,
760	     * etc.), we don't want the scroll to be reset. When this happens, we temporarily disable
761	     * the scroll mode.
762	     * However, we need to be careful here: the PullView system we use actually relies on
763	     * TRANSCRIPT_MODE_ALWAYS_SCROLL being active. So we add a new message in the queue to change
764	     * back the transcript mode once we've rendered the change.
765	     * <p>
766	     * (why is PullView dependent on this? When you show the keyboard, no event is being dispatched
767	     * to our application, but if we don't reset the scroll when the keyboard appears then you
768	     * could be looking at an element that isn't the latest one as you start scrolling down
769	     * [which will hide the keyboard] and start a very ugly animation revealing items currently
770	     * hidden. Fairly easy to test, remove the transcript mode from the XML and the .post() here,
771	     * then scroll in your history, display the keyboard and scroll again on your history)
772	     */
773	    @Override
774	    public void temporarilyDisableTranscriptMode() {
775	        list.setTranscriptMode(AbsListView.TRANSCRIPT_MODE_DISABLED);
776	        // Add a message to be processed after all current messages, to reset transcript mode to default
777	        list.post(() -> list.setTranscriptMode(AbsListView.TRANSCRIPT_MODE_ALWAYS_SCROLL));
778	    }
